            <ArticleTitle>Dissecting aneurysm of a right-sided descending aorta with a left-sided aortic arch.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>271-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 66-year-old man presented with myocardial infarction. Chest X-ray showed a large mediastinal mass. Aortic dissection was suggested by a past history of chest trauma, but the mass was in a very atypical site. Dissection of an abnormally placed right-sided descending aorta was confirmed by computed tomography. The aortic arch lay on the left side. This rare combination of congenital and acquired heart disease led to diagnostic difficulty.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
